g I suppose this  history and then putting it in some kind  of dialogue with what we know about the  rest of the world and then exploring  some of those similarities okay  differences okay so what led you to  write the book well you know a lot of  things in research already turned out by  surprise especially in anthropology  things sort of catch you by surprise or  your your things are unexpected so I've  been going to Russia and doing fieldwork  in this particular region that I that I  studied the Perim region which is sort  of near the near the Urals so not Moscow  not European Russia and not Siberia  either somewhere in the middle I've been  going there for 20 years and I lived  there in 2000-2001 for a whole year in a  village and I was researching  agriculture and religion and how things  had changed since the end of the Soviet  Union I wrapped up that project that was  my first book and I was looking for  something new to write about and new to  think about and this was the early  mid-2000s when oil was coming a big  thing this is an oil-producing region  friends I had were going to work in the  oil industry all of a sudden people I  knew were becoming fantastically rich if  they lived in or had some association  with an oil-producing region so it  seemed like an interesting thing to dig  into right and there is very little for  you to draw upon right so that's that  was kind of you know a bit of a surprise  I would imagine well that's right I mean  there's a lot of fantastic Russian  scholarship Russian language scholarship  you know local history and people there  writing about these things but there  really hasn't been a lot of stuff  written by academics in Western Europe  or the United States that takes into  account these ground-level things people  ready to pretend to write about the big  name things that you read about in  newspapers big company deals or Putin  struggles but the oligarchs but how oil  money flows in a particular town or how  the exchange of oil gets organized or  what company is sponsoring what museum  or these things that I'm interested in  people there we're talking about there  wasn't much to go on  you see and why do you think that is  well there is a couple things I think  you know I'm from discipline  anthropology that's very  fieldwork you go and live places and  hang out and try to understand things  from the inside and it's a fairly young  discipline with respect to Russia and  the former Soviet Union in the Cold War  this was not possible you couldn't go  sure yeah anthropologists were elsewhere  in the world so it's only really since  the 1990s that Eve that any anthropology  was possible in the former Soviet Union  so there's a sort of a generation ahead  of me but I but I'm the second  generation of people who've been able to  do fieldwork so there hasn't been much  time to do these things so that's one  thing and and you know anthropologists  have only really recently become  interested in oil and energy at all so  that is not that's interesting yeah I  mean anthropologists you know the the  you know there's been some studies but  but this has not been a major focus of  attention at least in recent years some  in this in the 70s oil crisis and and  then in the World War 2 era there were  sort of little boom watts of  anthropologists interested in energy but  it's in a big way has happened in the  past few years so I think the  combination of those two things now I am  curious why did you pick that particular  area of Russia out of every other place  in the world you could have studied well  that is also a tale of coincidence that  begins really when I was an  undergraduate at Middlebury College and  I was spending my junior year abroad and  wanting to learn about religion and  things like this then I met some people  in Moscow who said well we study  religion why don't you come with us this  summer we're going out to the perm  region to study you know religious  communities and I said all right why not  you know I was a junior in college I had  the summer free so I spent the summer  out in in the rural areas with the perm  region with this crowd  and that was 1994 and I have not stopped  going back since then so it's  coincidence followed by inertia so how  did you do the research for the book  tell us about the methodology so  anthropology is a largely not entirely  but largely a field work based  discipline you go and you talk to people  so it's very much based on interviews  some of them very formal  where I have sort of a set of questions  that I definitely want to ask somebody  but I also pay a lot of attention to  informal conversations or chats that I  have with people who I meet who are  either in the oil industry or in  government or given my interest in  Russian culture and cultural fields in  say they work in museums and so it's  very much based on interviews there's  also a lot of sort of newspaper and  media research for the older part the  Soviet part there's some archival  research but it's it's assembled from  these different pieces of sort of  empirical on the ground some of which  are structured buts a lot of which are  just fortuitous that they just they just  happen and and that has allowed me to  build up these larger right right so how  let's talk about some of your findings  and and actually in doing the research  did anything you come across say wow I  wasn't anticipating about or you know  very surprised and kind of thing oh all  the time I mean I mean you know that I I  guess I've sort of gotten used to it  because you know the unexpected things  are fairly common in fieldwork and  graphic research because life is a  fairly unexpected thing and what you  want to do is capture that and not edit  it out in as your you know consider data  or something but you know I was very  surprised by and interested in the way  in which the identity of the perm region  has become wrapped up in the oil  industry and how the oil industry has  become a big sponsor of local cultural  festivals and museums and in part this  is sort of an effort to blunt critiques  and respond to people's critiques of  their new wealth and and and things like  this but it's it's it's very deeply  implicated in very local cultural  understandings there's a lot of research  about you know Russian nationalism being  based on oil wealth and at the very high  sort of federal statewide countrywide  Russian things but what I was finding  was that most people's entry levels to  identity were not national they were in  large part very local connected to the  region or the district or very very  particular cultural traditions so that  was an interesting and somewhat  surprising finding  so what about some of your other  findings sure well going back to you  know one of the original premises of the  book I said was to was to put Russia in  comparison with other places right yes  so tell us so so here's one so here's  just one example of a few that I deal  with the post-soviet oil industry in the  Perim region in the 1990s was built  largely on barter non-monetized exchange  and there's nothing in the West and in  many of the Western or post-colonial oil  producing countries it's all about oil  money I mean there's no commodity that  is more closely associated with money we  see it at every gas station the price in  money of oil Russia in the 1990s there's  because of state crises and economic  catastrophe basically people are trading  oil without money and many other things  without money so this this new oil elite  in this new industry and the place of  oil in that regional state apparatus was  very much built without money and and  that even though money is now quite a  hugely important thing in the 2000s that  history is still important for the  structure of things and peoples  instincts and expectations so are they  still bartering today as well as some  what I mean not not nearly to the extent  of the 1990s but somewhat I mean if you  look at many of the international deals  that Russia does in oil and gas  particularly with former Soviet  countries from you know mulled over  through Ukraine into Central Asia many  of these are in fact barter or part  barter deals and to get back to the  global scene if you look harder and  people haven't because they expect oil  and money to be so close to there's  actually a lot of global oil trade that  is barter and I think that changes the  way we should look at the oil industry  on a global scale and it's a kind of  thing we see particularly while if we  look at 1990s Russia which was such a  clear example of this but then once you  start looking you find you know China in  Africa these days many of these do these  deals for oil and Africa made by Chinese  companies are barter based you give us  the oil we build the dam there's not a  monetary transaction and so thinking  through those kinds of things enables us  to see from the perspective of Russia  something new about  the whole picture so in the area that  you look at it in Russia tell me about  the population no would it be mostly  middle class upper middle class I mean  or is there more of a hierarchy low  middle you know right absolutely an  excellent question one of the clear  things that the oil industry has been  involved in changing it is so the perm  region is about 2 million people about a  million in the main city of Parham the  rest spread out through the region in  what used to be Soviet agricultural  towns and a lot of heavy industry  defense industry things like this in  Soviet period the coming of the oil  industry in the post-soviet period has  dramatically increased inequality as a  lot of wealth has been concentrated in  those districts that are associated with  the oil industry and the people who work  for the oil companies so you find  enormous levels of inequality that you  certainly didn't have in the Soviet  period in this in this region that are  that are really traceable to the coming  of a particularly kind of capitalist oil  industry so there's inequality in the  area you study absolutely ok so some  people just missed the boat in terms of  getting involved with you know oil  companies or well that's a part of it um  you know part of it is just fortuitous  if there's oil under you or not um you  know this is an old American story as  well right where's the oil and who gets  it and so that's some of it's just sort  of accident of geography some of it is  the fact that the oil industry is  particular in that it's a fairly low  labor industry it's it needs you need a  lot of money and a lot of high  technology to extract oil but once  you've dug the well you only need a few  people there they bring to you so so the  population involved in the oil industry  is very small I see it's not like a mine  where you'd have like whole segments of  population involved in interesting I  didn't realize that so then are the oil  companies then you had mentioned that  them sponsoring a lot of different kinds  of events are they putting money back  into the culture in terms of education  that kind of thing yes they certainly  are and and so and in all of these  domains health care  education culture whether it's enough or  whether it's public relations campaign  or whether it's really only papering  over inequality and ecological damage is  one of the items of debate in the Perim  region as elsewhere in the oil-producing  world in the u.s. upstate New York or  Nigeria or or elsewhere but certainly  that's there and one of the  particularity with the way this has  happened in the post-soviet period is  that that practice of corporate  sponsorship grew up right alongside the  re-emergence of the state and they're  very closely tied together this is the  part of the argument of the book is that  the oil company in the healthcare  industry is very closely allied with the  state government in the healthcare  industry same for culture same for  education so these things are very  closely entwined not just that the level  of say the federal budget in Moscow but  at the level of practice in particular  regions and cities where the oil  industry operates do you foresee any  change in the future it's very hard to  predict and I have spent much of the  semester embarrassing myself in front of  my students with predictions about  Ukraine and Crimea in which I'm  constantly proven wrong on a daily basis  so I think I will abstain from the  prediction industry for for now okay and  then you know in terms of what do you  conclude in the book ultimately well I  think one of the main conclusions of the  book I mean there's all these little  conclusions about how it's working and  what the comparisons are but one of the  main conclusions and one of the main  takeaway points is that we really  haven't understood Russia as an  oil-producing country as well as we  should have  we need to understand not just things  about the federal budget or tax policy  or what the size of the oil company's  reserves is but really how oil and  culture and society have become twined  together in contemporary Russia and I  think a better understanding of that is  fairly crucial for for our  understandings of Russia in the  post-soviet period very good thank you  so much for being here with us today and  sharing some of your work well thank you  it's been a pleasure to talk  you for more information about professor  Rogers and his research please visit our  website at yale.edu slash McMillan  report be sure to join us again for  another episode of the McMillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty McMillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale

